.TH TSK_TIMELINE 1
.SH NAME
tsk_timeline - Export a forensic timeline from a disk image, with optional filtering by timestamp type.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tsk_timeline [-vVm] [ -t
.I types
.B ] [ -i
.I imgtype
.B ] [ -b
.I dev_sector_size
.B ] [ -z
.I zone
.B ] [ -s
.I seconds
.B ] [ -k
.I password
.B ]
.I image [images]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tsk_timeline
examines each of the file systems in a disk image and exports a timeline in the mactime body file format (the same format produced by tsk_gettimes and 'fls -m').  The output can be used directly as input to mactime to make a timeline of file activity, and can be restricted to selected timestamp types with the \-t option.  Entries that have no timestamp of the selected types are omitted from the output.  The data is printed to STDOUT, which can then be redirected to a file.

The arguments are as follows:
.IP "-t types"
The timestamp types to include in the timeline, given as a combination of the letters
.B m
(last modification time),
.B a
(last access time),
.B c
(metadata change time) and
.B b
(creation / birth time).  For example, '\-t mb' exports only modification and creation times.  If not given, all timestamp types (macb) are exported and the output is identical to tsk_gettimes.
.IP -v
verbose output to stderr
.IP -V
Print version
.IP -m
Calculate the MD5 hash of each file and include it in the output (slow).
.IP "-i imgtype"
The format of the image file, such as raw.
Use '\-i list' to list the supported types.
If not given, autodetection methods are used.
.IP "-b dev_sector_size"
The size (in bytes) of the device sectors.
If not given, autodetection methods are used.
.IP "-k password"
Decryption password for encrypted volumes.
.IP "-s seconds"
The time skew of the original system in seconds.  For example, if the
original system was 100 seconds slow, this value would be \-100.
.IP "-z zone"
The ASCII string of the time zone of the original system.  For
example, EST or GMT.  These strings must be defined by your operating
system and may vary.
.IP "image [images]"
The disk or partition image to read, whose format is given with '\-i'.
Multiple image file names can be given if the image is split into multiple segments.
If only one image file is given, and its name is the first in a sequence (e.g., as indicated by ending in '.001'), subsequent image segments will be included automatically.

.SH EXAMPLES
To export a full timeline of image.dd:

	# tsk_timeline ./image.dd > body.txt

To export only modification and creation times and build a timeline with mactime:

	# tsk_timeline -t mb ./image.dd | mactime -b - > timeline.txt

